    "knowledge_point": "Utilizing the Periodicity Property of the Secant Function",
    "problem": "If $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\sqrt{2},$ then what is $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+6\\pi )?$",
    "worked_solution": "The period of $y=\\operatorname{sec}⁡x$ is $2\\pi .$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+2n\\pi ).\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=3$ into the right-hand-side of the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+2\\cdot 3\\cdot \\pi )=\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+6\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\operatorname{sec}⁡x=\\sqrt{2},$ then $\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+6\\pi )=\\sqrt{2}.$",
